    <!-- SEO content -->
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <h2>Texas Construction Mechanics Lien — Not a Vehicle Lien</h2>

      <p>If you searched for "Texas mechanics lien form" and you are looking for a form related to a motor vehicle — stop. The Texas DMV vehicle lien form (VTR-265-M) is processed through TxDMV and the Texas Department of Motor Vehicles. That form has nothing to do with construction.</p>

      <p>This page covers <strong>Texas mechanic's and materialman's liens under Texas Property Code Chapter 53</strong> — the legal tool that contractors, subcontractors, and material suppliers use to protect their right to payment for construction work on real property (land and buildings).</p>

      <p><strong>Note on terminology:</strong> Texas law uses "mechanic's and materialman's lien" (M&amp;M lien) rather than the generic "mechanics lien." The Affidavit Claiming a Lien under Tex. Prop. Code §53.052 is the document you file with the county clerk.</p>

      <p>A Texas mechanics lien (also called a mechanic's and materialman's lien) is a legal claim against real property under Texas Property Code Chapter 53, updated by HB 2237 (effective January 1, 2022). A recorded Texas M&amp;M lien attaches to the property's title, encumbering the owner's ability to sell or refinance until the lien is resolved.</p>

      <h2>Texas Property Code Chapter 53 — HB 2237 (2022 Update)</h2>

      <p>Texas Property Code Chapter 53 is the governing statute for construction liens on real property in Texas. It covers who can file, how, and when.</p>

      <p><strong>HB 2237 — Major 2022 Reforms:</strong> House Bill 2237 was signed into law in 2021 and took effect <strong>January 1, 2022</strong>. It represented the most significant overhaul of Texas lien law in decades. Key changes include:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Clearer monthly notice requirements</strong> for subcontractors and suppliers on residential projects</li>
        <li><strong>Simplified retainage claim procedures</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Updated form requirements</strong> for the Affidavit Claiming a Lien</li>
        <li><strong>Clarification of fund trapping provisions</strong> (protecting subcontractors when GC is paid)</li>
        <li><strong>Revised constitutional lien provisions</strong> for residential homestead property</li>
      </ul>

      <p>LienForm generates forms that comply with HB 2237 as in effect after January 1, 2022. Pre-2022 forms are outdated and may not satisfy the current statute.</p>

      <p><strong>Key statutory sections:</strong></p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Section</th>
            <th>Subject</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>§53.001</td><td>Definitions</td></tr>
          <tr><td>§53.021</td><td>Who may claim a lien (materialmen, laborers, contractors, architects, engineers)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>§53.052</td><td>Affidavit Claiming a Lien — form and requirements</td></tr>
          <tr><td>§53.053</td><td>Deadline to file the affidavit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>§53.056</td><td>Notice of lien claim to owner/GC</td></tr>
          <tr><td>§53.057</td><td>Monthly notices for subcontractors and suppliers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>§53.058</td><td>Retainage notice requirements</td></tr>
          <tr><td>§53.158</td><td>Deadline to sue to foreclose the lien — generally 1 year after the last day the affidavit could have been timely filed under §53.052, not necessarily 1 year from your actual filing date (for contracts entered on or after Jan. 1, 2022)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Deadlines by Role</h2>

      <p>Texas lien deadlines are based on calendar months, not day-counts, which makes them somewhat unusual. The deadline is always the <strong>15th day of a specified month</strong> after the month in which work was last performed.</p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Role</th>
            <th>Project Type</th>
            <th>Deadline</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Original Contractor (GC)</td>
            <td>Residential</td>
            <td>15th day of the 3rd month after the month work was last furnished</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Original Contractor (GC)</td>
            <td>Commercial</td>
            <td>15th day of the 4th month after the month work was last furnished</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Subcontractor / Supplier</td>
            <td>Residential</td>
            <td>15th day of the 3rd month after the month work was last furnished</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Subcontractor / Supplier</td>
            <td>Commercial</td>
            <td>15th day of the 4th month after the month work was last furnished</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Retainage claimant (any)</td>
            <td>Residential or Commercial</td>
            <td>30 days after the earlier of: owner's notice of completion OR contract termination</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p><strong>How to calculate:</strong></p>

      <p>If you last furnished labor or materials in <strong>October</strong>, count forward to determine your deadline month:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>GC or subcontractor (residential): October is month 0 → 3rd month forward = January → <strong>January 15</strong></li>
        <li>GC or subcontractor (commercial): October is month 0 → 4th month forward = February → <strong>February 15</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <p>The month count depends on <strong>project type</strong>, not role — a general contractor and a subcontractor on the same residential project share the same deadline.</p>

      <p><strong>What is "last furnishing"?</strong> The last calendar month in which you performed work or delivered materials. Warranty calls and minor touch-up work may or may not count — consult an attorney for borderline situations.</p>

      <p>LienForm's deadline calculator asks for your role, project type, and last furnishing month, then calculates the exact date automatically.</p>

      <h2>Monthly notices for residential and nonresidential work</h2><p>For contracts subject to the current section 53.056 rules, qualifying non-original claimants generally notify the owner and original contractor by the fifteenth of the second month after residential furnishing or the third month after nonresidential furnishing. Check the contract date, service, statutory wording, and any special retained-funds provisions.</p><p>These notices are separate from filing the lien affidavit. The current preparation tool does not generate Texas monthly-notice or retainage-notice templates. Obtain the applicable statutory notice separately.</p><p><a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/PR/htm/PR.53.htm#53.056">Texas Property Code section 53.056</a> controls the monthly notice discussed here.</p><h2>Filing with the County Clerk</h2>

      <p>In Texas, mechanics liens are filed with the <strong>county clerk</strong> — not a county recorder or register of deeds. Texas uses county clerks for real property records.</p>

      <p>The document you file is an <strong>Affidavit Claiming a Lien</strong> under Tex. Prop. Code §53.052. This affidavit must contain:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>The name and address of the claimant</li>
        <li>The name of the property owner (as known to the claimant)</li>
        <li>The name of the party who hired the claimant (owner, GC, or sub)</li>
        <li>A description of the labor or materials furnished</li>
        <li>The amount claimed</li>
        <li>A description of the property</li>
        <li>The claimant's signature, notarized</li>
      </ol>

      <p><strong>Notarization is required.</strong> Unlike some other states, Texas requires the Affidavit Claiming a Lien to be signed and notarized before filing with the county clerk. LienForm's Texas bundle includes instructions for notarization and a signature block formatted for Texas requirements.</p>

      <h2>County Clerk Offices</h2>

      <p>File with the county clerk in the county where the property is located:</p>

      <p>Ask the property county clerk for current recording fees, formatting, office details, and approved electronic submission methods. A statewide fee estimate is not a reliable filing budget.</p>

      <p>Filing fees in Texas vary by county and are typically charged per document or per page. Contact the county clerk directly or check their website for current fee schedules. Many larger Texas counties accept electronic filing through county portals or approved third-party systems.</p>

      <h2>Retainage claims require a separate review</h2><p>Do not assume the ordinary invoice notice and affidavit calendar resolves retained-funds claims. Review the applicable contract date and sections 53.057, 53.101–53.105, and 53.103, along with the contract and completion or termination records. Arrange Texas legal review before relying on a retained-funds claim.</p><p>The current preparation tool does not produce a Texas retainage-notice template.</p><p><strong>Prepare your Texas mechanics lien draft — $24.99:</strong> Preview project facts and download a claim draft with supporting deadline and filing information. Monthly-notice and retainage-notice templates are not included. Review statutory form, service, and execution requirements before using the document.</p>

      <p>No account required. Instant download.</p>

      <p><em>Content reviewed for accuracy against Texas Property Code Chapter 53, as updated by HB 2237 (effective January 1, 2022). This is not legal advice. Consult a licensed Texas attorney for advice specific to your situation.</em></p>

      <p>Texas's month-based deadline (the 15th of a specified calendar month) is one of the more unusual rules covered on this site — <a href="/mechanics-lien/michigan/">Michigan</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/california/">California</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/arizona/">Arizona</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/georgia/">Georgia</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/ohio/">Ohio</a>, and <a href="/mechanics-lien/washington/">Washington</a> have different triggering events and deadline rules. See the <a href="/mechanics-lien/form/">mechanics lien form guide</a> if you're not sure which state's rules apply to your project.</p>

